 ## Subtopics
 
-### VIRIM Architecture
-The VIRIM (Volume Image Rendering in Motion) architecture is a specific implementation of this method characterized by flexibility and object-order ray casting [S1]. It uses a farm of OSP (Object Space Processor) processors for the ray-casting portion, while fixed functions like slice reconstruction are handled by dedicated hardware units [S1]. A key distinction is that VIRIM replicates the entire volume memory in each parallel geometry unit to circumvent data dependency problems, whereas sibling systems like VOGUE partition the dataset [S1]. It allows users to implement different visualization models using programmable ray-casting processors to balance image quality and speed [S1].
+### [VIRIM Architecture](3147-virim-architecture.md)
+VIRIM is an object-order volume rendering engine developed at the University of Mannheim and commercially distributed by Volume Graphics GmbH. It implements projection plane raycasting, where rays belonging to a particular scanline reside on the same projection plane and are resampled in top-to-bottom order. The architecture is characterized by its flexibility, utilizing a farm of programmable Digital Signal Processors (DSPs) for the ray-casting portion of the algorithm, allowing users to implement different visualization models and balance image quality versus rendering speed. Unlike VOGUE, which partitions the dataset, VIRIM replicates the entire volume memory in each parallel geometry unit to circumvent data dependency problems.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
-*   **Memory Bandwidth:** Achieves up to 640 MBytes/second using commercial DRAMs with Fast Page Mode and alternate bank read-out [S1].
-*   **Data Limits:** Original dataset size is limited to $256^3$ (16-bit voxels) with 4Mbit ORAMs or $512 \times 512 \times 256$ with 16Mbit DRAMs. Resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High speed for geometry operations; flexibility in visualization models via programmable processors; simple grey value segmentation via LUTs [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Fixed gradient estimation hardware limits flexibility in shading models compared to fully programmable units; memory replication increases hardware cost [S1].
+*   **Performance:** A single geometry unit can generate 26 to 36 million transformed locations per second using true 3D resampling [S1].
+*   **Memory Bandwidth:** The dedicated memory system achieves data access rates of up to 640 MBytes/second (calculated as 40 MHz $\times$ 8 neighbors $\times$ 2 Bytes/voxel) [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The method offloads geometry calculations from the CPU to specialized hardware, reducing software computation time by approximately 80% for these operations [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** The maximal resampled dataset size is limited to $512^3$ due to gradient buffer constraints [S1]. The use of 6-bit fractional resolution for weights can introduce discretization artifacts, though they are designed to be visually imperceptible [S1].
+*   **Precision:** All calculations (density and gradient) are performed with 16-bit precision [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This method was developed as part of the VIRIM project, a volume ray-casting accelerator designed for flexibility and high performance [S1]. It was described in the 1995 paper "Three Architectures for Volume Rendering" by the Heidelberg Raytracer team [S1]. The architecture was designed to address the high computational cost of geometry operations, which accounted for 80% of the computation time in software implementations [S1].
+This method originates from the Heidelberg Raytracer and the VIRIM (Volume Rendering Image Machine) architecture [S1]. It was developed to address the high computational cost of geometry operations in volume rendering, specifically perspective calculations and resampling [S1]. The approach was detailed in publications around 1995, highlighting the use of custom hardware for these intensive tasks [S1].
 
